cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference this software, please cite it as below."
title: "power-content-check"
abstract: >-
  A deterministic conformance checker for California Power Content Labels.
  It checks a published label against the prescribed label format and makes
  no judgment about a supplier's power mix, performance, or compliance status.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Kelly-Reif"
    given-names: "Chelsea"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ChelseaKR/power-content-check"
license: Apache-2.0
# `version` mirrors the in-development version in pyproject.toml, which is the single
# source of truth; tests/test_release_claims.py asserts the two agree.
#
# `date-released` was absent for as long as nothing was tagged. It had carried 2026-08-18
# while `git tag --list` was empty here and on origin and no GitHub Release existed, which
# gave anyone citing this tool a release date for a release that never happened; the date
# was real, it was just the date of something else. It is back because `v0.1.0` was cut on
# the date below, and the date below is the date of that tag. tests/test_release_claims.py
# ties this field to a tag naming the declared version in both directions, so it could not
# have returned early and cannot be dropped now.
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-09-07
keywords:
  - california
  - power source disclosure
  - power content label
  - conformance
  - civic technology
